import json,pathlib
r='frontier-40-geometry-braids-rep-27';p=pathlib.Path('research')/f'{r}-batch-25.pages.json';a=json.loads(p.read_text());src={'title':'The Stacks Project, Resolution of Surfaces, Lemmas 54.7.3–8 (complete proofs read; normal-surface arguments reconstructed)','url':'https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/download/resolve.pdf'}
def row(id,title,deps,statement,strategy):return dict(id=id,kind='lemma',title=title,deps=deps,statement=statement,strategy=strategy,design_row='AG-BIR-1',local_addition=True,provenance={'statement':'literature-derived','proof':'ai-altered'},sources={'references':[src]},dependency_level=0)
base=['def-axiom-of-choice','def-dependent-choice','def-normal-surface-modification-and-normalized-point-blowup','lem-local-normal-surface-modification-dimension-and-projective-cohomology']
h1=row('lem-normal-surface-fibre-divisor-conormal-degree-positive','Positive conormal degree for a fibre divisor on a normal surface',base+['lem-normal-domain-implies-s-two','cor-regular-quotient-cohen-macaulay-equivalence','cor-cohen-macaulay-modules-have-no-embedded-associated-primes','thm-one-dimensional-regular-local-rings-are-dvrs','lem-normal-domain-implies-r-one','lem-nonzero-section-vanishing-at-a-point-has-positive-degree','cor-degree-additive-proper-curve'],r'''Assume AC and DC. Let $A$ be a normal Noetherian local domain of dimension two and $f:X\to\operatorname{Spec}A$ a normal integral modification. For any nonempty effective Cartier divisor $Z$ supported in the special fibre, some integral component $C$ of $Z$ satisfies $\deg_C(\mathcal O_X(-Z)|_C)>0$. In particular its conormal bundle is not trivial.''',r'''Write the one-dimensional fibre components $C_j$ and choose closed points $x_j$ off all other components. Such points exist in the nonempty open of each finite-type proper residue-field curve. Choose $g_j\in\mathfrak m_{X,x_j}$ with nonzero image in $\mathcal O_{C_j,x_j}$. In the common function field write $g_j=a_j/b_j$ with $a_j,b_j\in A$ and set $u=\prod a_j$. Each $a_j$ lies in the maximal ideal: otherwise $g_jb_j=a_j$ would make $g_j$ a unit. Thus $u$ has positive orders $e_j$ at the generic DVRs of all fibre curves, and $u=g_jh_j$ locally at $x_j$. Put $d_j=v_{C_j}(Z)$. Choose $i$ maximizing $d_i/e_i$ and rescale $u\mapsto u^{d_i}$, $Z\mapsto e_iZ$. The new orders satisfy $v_{C_j}(u)\ge v_{C_j}(Z)$ for all $j$, with equality at $i$. Normal surface local rings are CM; their effective Cartier quotients are CM, because a nonzerodivisor extends to a parameter tuple by the support-dimension argument, and hence have no embedded associated points. The image of $u$ in $\mathcal O_Z$ is zero at every generic associated point, hence zero, so $u$ is a section of the Cartier ideal $I$. Its restriction to $C_i$ is nonzero generically by equality of orders. Near $x_i$ only $C_i$ occurs in $Z$; the factor $g_i$ (raised to the rescaling power if necessary) is nonzero at its sole associated generic point, so is a nonzerodivisor on $\mathcal O_Z$. If $I=(t)$, $g_ih_i\in(t)$ therefore implies $h_i\in(t)$. Thus $u/t=g_i(h_i/t)$ vanishes at $x_i$. The corrected proper-integral-curve section/degree lemma gives positive degree. Tensor-power additivity removes the positive rescaling factor. This proves the normal-surface version; the original regular-surface helper was not silently applied outside its Statement.''')
h2=row('lem-projective-normal-surface-modification-h1-injects-off-special-fibre','H1 of a normal surface modification injects off its special fibre',base+['lem-surface-finite-type-normalization-finite',h1['id'],'lem-finite-over-projective-noetherian-affine-base-is-projective','thm-proper-pushforward-coherent'],r'''Assume AC and DC. Let $A$ be a normal two-dimensional Noetherian local domain essentially of finite type over a field or complete equicharacteristic local base, and let $X\to\operatorname{Spec}A$ be a projective normal modification. If $U$ is the inverse image of the punctured spectrum, $H^1(X,\mathcal O_X)\to H^1(U,\mathcal O_U)$ is injective.''',r'''Use the two-affine cover to represent a class by a Čech cocycle and glue the rank-two bundle $E$ with upper triangular transition matrix having that cocycle off the diagonal. This gives $0\to\mathcal O_X\to E\to\mathcal O_X\to0$, whose splitting is equivalent to vanishing of the class; the same construction on a refinement detects its restriction to $U$. The quotient defines a section $\sigma$ of the projective bundle $P(E)\to X$. A splitting over $U$ defines a disjoint second section $\sigma'$ there. Let $X'$ be the integral schematic closure of $\sigma'(U)$. If it missed $\sigma(X)$, $X'\to X$ would be both proper and affine, since the complement of the section is locally an affine-line chart. Proper coherent pushforward would then make its affine algebra finite. Being birational onto normal $X$, it is an isomorphism by the integral-closure argument, giving a global disjoint second section and a splitting, contrary to a nonzero class. Thus $X'$ meets $\sigma(X)$. Normalize $X'$ finitely using the normalization-finiteness helper. Its pullback $Z$ of the Cartier section is nonempty, supported in the special fibre, and effective Cartier because the integral dominant component is not contained in the section. The section's conormal bundle is $\mathcal O_X$: in a local splitting of $E$, its ideal is the coordinate of the other affine-line direction, and the kernel and quotient of the bundle extension are both $\mathcal O_X$. Pullback of this invertible Cartier ideal shows the conormal bundle of $Z$ is trivial. This contradicts the preceding positive-conormal-degree lemma on the normal modified surface. Hence every class vanishing off the special fibre is zero. The projective bundle and its normalization are projective over $A$ by twisting their coherent generators and by finite-projective composition, so all used two-affine/finite-type hypotheses hold.''')
h3=row('lem-normal-surface-modification-no-derived-residue-map','No derived residue map into structure cohomology of a normal surface modification',base+[h2['id'],'lem-r-one-s-two-intersection-of-height-one-localisations','lem-normal-domain-implies-s-two','thm-completion-of-a-noetherian-local-ring'],r'''Assume AC and DC. For $A,X$ as in the preceding injection lemma, with residue field $\kappa$, $\operatorname{Hom}_{D(A)}(\kappa[-1],R\Gamma(X,\mathcal O_X))=0$.''',r'''A degreewise finite free resolution $P$ of $\kappa$ gives the derived adjunction explicitly: apply sheaf/global-section Hom adjunction termwise to $P[-1]\otimes_A\mathcal O_X$ and a bounded-below injective resolution of $\mathcal O_X$. Thus the Hom group equals $\operatorname{Hom}_{D(X)}(L f^*\kappa[-1],\mathcal O_X)$. The source has cohomology at most degree one; its degree-one sheaf is $\mathcal O_{X_s}$, and its degree-zero sheaf is annihilated by the maximal ideal. Maps from the latter to the torsion-free $\mathcal O_X$ are zero, and lower cohomology gives no degree-zero maps by truncation. The truncation triangle therefore identifies the Hom group with $\operatorname{Ext}^1_X(\mathcal O_{X_s},\mathcal O_X)$. Regard a class as an extension $0\to\mathcal O_X\to E\to\mathcal O_{X_s}\to0$. Pulling back along $\mathcal O_X\twoheadrightarrow\mathcal O_{X_s}$ gives an extension of $\mathcal O_X$ by itself; it is split off the special fibre and hence globally split by the preceding H1 injection. Therefore $1\in\mathcal O_{X_s}$ lifts to a global section $s$ of $E$. Multiplying $s$ by the fibre ideal $I$ gives $I\to\mathcal O_X$. Since global structure functions are $A$ and global fibre-ideal functions are its maximal ideal $\mathfrak m$, this induces $\mathfrak m\to A$. It is multiplication by an element of the fraction field; at every height-one localization $\mathfrak m_\mathfrak p=A_\mathfrak p$, so that element belongs to $A_\mathfrak p$. Normal S2 intersection gives membership in $A$. Subtract this element from $s$. The new lift is annihilated by $I$, hence defines a splitting $\mathcal O_{X_s}\to E$ of the original extension. The Ext-one/extension correspondence here follows by an injective resolution: push out along the injection into the first injective and lift the quotient; it needs enough injectives, not enough projective module sheaves. This avoids misusing a module-only balanced-Ext assertion for sheaves.''')
h4=row('lem-projective-normal-surface-grauert-riemenschneider-vanishing','Grauert–Riemenschneider vanishing for the required normal surface modifications',base+[h3['id'],'lem-normal-projective-surface-dualizing-module-over-regular-local-base','lem-finite-length-duality-over-a-regular-local-base','lem-finite-regular-base-algebra-dualizing-biduality','thm-proper-pushforward-coherent'],r'''Assume AC and DC. Let $R$ be regular Noetherian local of dimension two, $A$ a finite normal local $R$-domain, and $X\to\operatorname{Spec}A$ a projective normal modification, in the permitted finite-type/completion class. For its normalized dualizing module $\omega_X$ over $\omega_A=\operatorname{Hom}_R(A,R)$, $H^1(X,\omega_X)=0$.''',r'''The preceding geometric/cohomology helper gives no cohomology above degree one; proper coherence and the isomorphism off the closed point make $H^1(X,\omega_X)$ finite length. If nonzero, Nakayama and a residue-field functional give a nonzero map from that module to $\kappa$. Truncation yields a nonzero map $\alpha:R\Gamma(X,\omega_X[2])\to\kappa[1]$. Finite-base coherent biduality makes derived dualization with $D_A=\omega_A[2]$ faithful on bounded finite complexes. Finite-length duality takes the simple residue module to another simple module with the same annihilator, hence to $\kappa$ up to isomorphism. Projective surface duality and homothety identify the dual of $R\Gamma(X,\omega_X[2])$ with $R\Gamma(X,\mathcal O_X)$. The dual of $\alpha$ is therefore a nonzero map $\kappa[-1]\to R\Gamma(X,\mathcal O_X)$, contradicting the preceding derived-residue-map lemma. Thus $H^1(X,\omega_X)=0$. Every duality arrow used is the actual evaluation/trace pairing over the regular subring, and no general proper duality theorem is cited in place of its proof.''')
for h in [h1,h2,h3,h4]:
 idx=next(i for i,x in enumerate(a[0]['items']) if x['id']=='thm-resolution-of-normal-surface-singularities');a[0]['items'].insert(idx,h)
